// REJECTS: Unexpected token
//
// `[1..]` is not a multiplicity. Legend spells an unbounded upper bound `[1..*]`; the range
// form requires BOTH bounds, and the bare '..' has no meaning.
//
// Worth pinning because '..' is a real token (DOT_DOT) and because the open-ended range is
// idiomatic in several neighbouring languages -- Rust, Kotlin, Perl slices. A rewrite that
// parses multiplicity as "lower, optional '..' , optional upper" accepts this and has to
// invent an upper bound, silently turning [1..] into [1..*] or into [1..1] depending on
// which default the author picked. Both are wrong and neither announces itself.
###Pure
Class fixture::neg::OpenUpperBound
{
   code: String[1..];
}
